{{also|Equip.}}
==English==

===Etymology===
From {{der|en|fr|équiper||to [[supply]], [[fit out]]}}, originally said of a [[ship]], {{der|en|fro|esquiper||to [[embark]]}}; of {{der|en|gem}} origin, most probably from {{der|en|non|skipa|t=to man (a ship)}}, from {{der|en|gem-pro|*skipōną||to ship, sail, embark}}. 

Compare with {{cog|gmh|schipfen}}, {{cog|de|schiffern}}, {{cog|is|skipa}}, {{cog|ang|scipian}}. {{dbt|en|ship}}.

===Pronunciation===
* {{IPA|en|/ɪˈkwɪp/}}
* {{audio|en|LL-Q1860 (eng)-Vealhurl-equip.wav|a=Southern England}}
* {{IPA|en|/ekˈjuːp/|a=Indic|q=proscribed}}
* {{rhymes|en|ɪp|s=2}}
* {{hyph|en|equip}}

===Verb===
{{en-verb|++|past_ptc2=equipt|past_ptc2_qual=archaic}}

# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[supply]] with something necessary in order to carry out a specific action or task; to provide with (e.g. weapons, provisions, munitions, rigging).
#* '''1698-1699''', {{w|Edmund Ludlow}}, ''Memoirs''
#*: Gave orders for '''equipping''' a considerable fleet.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1916|chapter=s:Indicator Tells Pursuing Police Speed of Automobile|title=Popular Science Monthly/Volume 88
|passage=A semicircular plate, with the numbers in multiples of five up to thirty miles an hour, is '''equipped''' with a pointer, which indicates accurately the speed of the car.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|author=Randall Stross|title=Wireless Internet for All, Without the Towers|magazine=w:The New York Times|url=https://www.nytimes.com/2007/02/04/business/yourmoney/04digi.html?_r=4|date=2007-02-04|passage=Some of those neighbors will also have Meraki boxes that serve as repeaters, relaying the signal still farther to more neighbors. The company '''equips''' its boxes with software that maintains a “mesh network,” which dynamically reroutes signals as boxes are added or unplugged, and as environmental conditions that affect network performance fluctuate moment to moment.}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|passage=More than 40,000 would-be migrants to Europe have been intercepted at sea since Italy began paying and '''equipping''' the Libyan coastguard to do so in 2017.|url=https://www.telegraph.co.uk/news/2020/02/13/have-lost-four-years-life-desperate-migrants-stuck-squalid-libyan/|date=2020-02-13|author=Roland Oliphant|work=w:The Telegraph|title='I have lost four years of my life': the desperate migrants stuck in squalid Libyan camps}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To [[dress up]]; to [[array]]; to [[clothe]].
#* {{RQ:Spectator|I|129|2 July 1711|passage=The country are led astray in following the town, and '''equipped''' in a ridiculous habit, when they fancy themselves in the height of the mode.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive}} To prepare (someone) with a skill.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=1921|author=w:Rafael Sabatini|title=s:In Destiny's Clutch
|passage=it is no more than proper that you should '''equip''' us with a vessel in which to pursue the journey which you interrupted}}
#* {{quote-journal|en|date=1947-10-20|title=Russian Catechism|journal=Time|passage=Already in the primary school work is conducted for the purpose of '''equipping''' the pupils with those elements of general knowledge which are closely related to the military preparation of future warriors.}}
# {{lb|en|transitive|gaming}} To equip oneself with (an item); to bring (equipment) into active use.
#* {{quote-text|en|year=2002|author=Prima Temp Authors|title=PlayStation 2: Hot Strategies for Cool Games|page=69
|passage=Take it down from a distance with a magic spell, or '''equip''' your sword and attack it at close range.}}

